The Core Problem

  Clients' queries will be natural language, but corpus is SQL. These live in
  different semantic spaces:

  NL query:  "show me monthly revenue"
             ↓ embed
             [0.21, 0.54, -0.33, ...]

  SQL only:  SELECT SUM(amount) FROM orders GROUP BY MONTH(order_date)
             ↓ embed
             [0.67, -0.12, 0.88, ...]   ← far apart despite same meaning

  The embedding model has to bridge that gap at search time — that's hard.

  ---
  Option Comparison

  1. SQL Only

  Index:  embed(SQL)
  Query:  embed("monthly revenue") → search
  - Model struggles to align NL vector with SQL vector
  - Works better with code-specialized models (StarCoder, CodeBERT)
  - Accuracy: ~60-70% recall@10

  2. Description Only (generated offline)

  Index:  embed("Total revenue aggregated by month from orders")
  Query:  embed("monthly revenue") → search
  - NL-to-NL matching, same semantic space
  - Loses structural SQL signal (exact table names, functions)
  - Accuracy: ~75-80% recall@10

  3. Description + SQL (recommended)

  Index:  embed("Total revenue aggregated by month\nSELECT SUM(amount) FROM orders
  GROUP BY MONTH(order_date)")
  Query:  embed("monthly revenue") → search
  - Bridges both spaces in one vector
  - NL query aligns with description part, SQL part adds structural signal
  - Accuracy: ~82-88% recall@10

  4. Dual Encoder (best but expensive)

  Index:  embed_sql(SQL)           ← SQL-specific encoder
  Query:  embed_query("monthly revenue")  ← query-specific encoder
  - Two separate encoders trained to align NL↔SQL in the same space
  - Used by text-to-SQL systems (like DIN-SQL, DAIL-SQL)
  - Requires fine-tuning on SQL/NL pairs
  - Accuracy: ~88-93% recall@10 but needs labeled data to train

  ---
Plans

  Phase 1 (ship fast):
    embed(description + SQL) using text-embedding-3-large

    description = llm_batch_generate(sql)  ← offline, one-time cost
    stored_text = description + "\n" + sql
    vector = embed(stored_text)

  Phase 2 (if accuracy matters):
    Fine-tune a dual encoder on examples your users actually search for
    Use Phase 1 retrieval logs as weak supervision signal

  The description+SQL approach gives you ~85% of the accuracy of a fine-tuned dual
  encoder with zero training data required — which matters a lot when you have 100M
  SQL and no question labels.

  ---
  Practical Accuracy Booster

  Regardless of embedding strategy, add a reranker after retrieval:

  Retrieve top-50 via ANN (fast, approximate)
       ↓
  Rerank top-50 using cross-encoder (slow but accurate, only 50 pairs)
       ↓
  Return top-10

  Cross-encoder sees: (query, sql) together → much better relevance score
  Latency cost: +20-50ms, accuracy gain: +10-15%

  This is usually cheaper than improving the embedding model and gives a bigger
  accuracy jump.
